{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'cat .flow/specs/fn-247-one-language-for-the-framework-a-rust.md'","aggregated_output":"# One language for the framework: Rust process ownership and Cargo development tools\n\n> Local HTML render lens: open `.flow/artifacts/fn-247-one-language-for-the-framework-a-rust/spec.html` locally — regenerable; Markdown is the record. <!-- flow-next:artifact-link -->\n\n## Conversation Evidence\n\n> user (2026-10-24): \"why is there backends/tla and backends/tlc ?\" then, on moving process supervision out of the emitter: \"yes\"\n> user (2026-10-24): \"can w delete all __pycache__ folders?\"\n> user (2026-10-24): \"why do we have python scripts at all?\"\n> user (2026-10-24): \"yes file\"\n> user (2026-10-24): \"why need compatability? feel free to make breaking changes from pytjon driver\"\n> user (2026-10-24): \"can we drop byte-for-byte compatability from open tasks to speed things up? we don't need backwards compatabiltiy\"\n> user (2026-10-24): \"yes apply savings\" to one timed required-backend validation pipeline, focused intermediate checks and frozen milestone bookkeeping during validation.\n\n## Goal & Context\n\nRemove Python from the active Model/framework development toolchain while preserving Model semantics and operational safety under native Rust tool contracts. Shared process ownership belongs in `umpire-backend-process`, separate from TLA+ emission. A host-only `xtask` replaces build helpers, their embedded Python glue and controls. The retained-original Python hash verifier is retired, not ported; the Rust lifecycle comparison stays and no retained artifact changes. Go stays where the host requires it; Bash remains orchestration.\n\nDevelopers use documented native Rust driver contracts, with callers updated together. Keep cheap useful happy-path invocations; no aliases, diagnostic spellings or old encodings are required solely for backward compatibility. The post-fn-237.3 integrated guest is semantic reference evidence, not a byte oracle. This work changes no Model, checker meaning, protocol, IR or Temporal feature behavior.\n\n## Architecture & Data Models\n\n`umpire-backend-process` owns the existing `Limits`, `Refusal`, `Scratch`, `ProcessFailure`, Java admission, process execution and bounded-text contracts. TLA+, TLC and Apalache use it directly where they need those contracts; Apalache acquires no TLC dependency. TLA+ retains binding, emission, Snowcat and typed-value admission. Update callers coherently where the native interface differs; preserve functional typed failure/status mapping and all process safety controls. Old signatures, compatibility reexports and diagnostic wording are not acceptance criteria solely for backward compatibility.\n\nUse an allocation-free process supervisor inside the process crate. Prepare executable resolution, argument/environment bytes, descriptors and bounded buffers before forking. Every path in the forked supervisor and its pre-exec target child is restricted to audited libc calls and stack-only scalar/byte operations; it cannot allocate, format, lock, run Rust destructors, unwind or call general `std` process/filesystem helpers. All child exits use `_exit`. This is the production path for focused Cargo tests and library consumers; no sibling executable, runtime Cargo invocation or warm workspace build is required. A standalone Rust helper is an alternative only if a reviewed build/discovery contract first proves all those use cases.\n\nLinux retains the private supervisor group, target session, subreaper and adopted-descendant cleanup, including escaped process groups. Darwin retains non-root admission and descendant prevention. Other platforms refuse at admission. Signals and owner-liveness EOF preserve the existing outcome mapping. Safe Rust retains capture handles, inode checks, output concatenation, dump admission and scratch cleanup. After the supervisor proof, scratch directories use `tempfile::Builder` and parent-side syscalls use `rustix` safe wrappers; the forked child's async-signal-safe path stays raw libc. Limits keep their existing units and enforcement: Java heap configuration is not an OS memory cap, and polling a regular output file is not a strict kernel output cap.\n\n`xtask` is a native development-profile binary, excluded from guest/wasm dependency closures. Its commands use documented native Rust arguments, JSON schemas, status/error rendering and TOML behavior; retain useful ordinary invocations without emulating Python quirks. It hashes in-process with `sha2` (no `shasum` subprocess), encodes JSON with plain `serde_json` (default BTreeMap-backed `Map`; `preserve_order` off workspace-wide), and handles OS arguments with `std::env::args_os`/`OsString` (no clap), decoding command tokens only where needed. Internal commands replace cache-key calculation, launcher publication and Cargo-artifact selection formerly embedded in the guest shell. Compile xtask before setting guest wrapper/encoded flags; the compiler wrapper directly executes the built native tool, never recursively starts Cargo. Preserve raw compiler argv, previous-wrapper execution, jobserver descriptors and macOS loader environment.\n\n## API Contracts\n\nPreserve `run_process` and cancellable execution's distinction between refusal, child status and retained output. Preserve timeout/cancellation output, first-signal handling, owner EOF, target signal mapping, `125` owner-loss status and `128+signal` outcomes as applicable. Diagnostic text may follow the native interface; callers must still distinguish typed refusals, launch failures and child outcomes.\n\nGuest identities, hash inputs and sidecar records use native Rust schemas and remain deterministic, portable and stable going forward. Invalidate stale caches when encoding changes instead of supporting old formats. Sidecars publish before compilation and disappear on failure. Closure identity retains checksums, non-dev edges across all platforms, build dependencies, edge structure, ambiguity and shared-feature unification behavior.\n\nGuest check mode distinguishes fresh, stale/missing and tool/build/discovery failure, reports useful differences and never publishes the candidate. Ordinary useful status values may remain; old Python formatting/first-offset rendering is not required. Missing prerequisites and probe/metadata/build/staleness failures are explicit failures under the native driver contract, regardless of CI presence. This helper policy change does not change R5 backend default/skip behavior.\n\nRust/Cargo and the documented mise toolchain are prerequisites. Freshness enters through a minimal Bash bootstrap launcher that sets `MISE_AUTO_INSTALL=false` and `MISE_EXEC_AUTO_INSTALL=false` before its first probe. Missing bootstrap tools and bootstrap compilation errors fail clearly without guest build or publication. Bootstrap xtask before guest wrapper/flags are installed; Rust then owns authoritative TOML/JSON parsing, installed-tool metadata, pin consistency, Rust/rustup/Go/protoc/rust-src/wasm readiness and freshness validation. Do not duplicate semantic validators in Bash or retain the old local/CI missing-tool skip matrix. An existing cache must not bypass native validation. Prove the actual cold Make/shell entrypoint, disabled auto-install from the first probe, fail-fast/compiler-failure behavior and never-publish check mode.\n\nThe retained-original hash integrity check is thrown away (owner, 2026-10-24): `verify.py` is removed, not ported, and `manifest.json` (paths and hashes) is deleted. The originals stay in Git as historical evidence; the completed fn-227 checksum artifact under `.flow/artifacts` is history and is not consulted. The compatibility checkpoint keeps its semantic lifecycle comparison against `original/rows.snap.txt` and its mutation control.\n\n## Edge Cases & Constraints\n\nExecution admission requires fn-237.3 done, with its implementation and Flow closure commits present in `umpire` ancestry. Begin from that integrated tip and record its guest hash as reference evidence before changing tools. A native ready result alone does not clear this gate. Do not add a whole-fn-237 dependency: its later tasks follow this spec and fn-246. Preserve fn-246's whole-spec dependency on this spec. The separate fn-246/whole-fn-243 scheduling correction belongs to that plan.\n\nPromote `libc` 0.2.191 and, under the conductor's explicit autonomous R18 amendment, the already resolved `toml` 1.1.8+spec-1.1.0 for host-only xtask parsing. The latter retains checksum `20489e00e4d8741d6be680764cc12e270655e375a20d1011e844a9c3379e678d`, disables default features and requests only existing `std`, `parse` and `serde` features. New dependencies are admitted under fn-210 R18 (a release within 12 months, or finished and very widely used). Admitted here: `tar` 0.4.46 (default features off, no xattr) and `flate2` 1.1.10 (miniz_oxide backend) for the installer; `sha2` 0.11 for xtask hashing; `tempfile` 3.27 and `rustix` 1.1 (already resolved) for the process crate. Record before/after package and feature inventories, including the guest closure and each new transitive package's R18 basis; host-only additions never enter a guest closure, and `cargo deny check bans` passes. Keep workspace `unsafe_code=forbid`. The process crate alone may follow the existing runtime precedent: mirror the workspace lint policy with crate-local `unsafe_code=deny`, allowing unsafe only in its audited syscall module. All other modules forbid unsafe; no workspace-wide relaxation is allowed. Reuse safe standard-library facilities where their behavior is equivalent and use syscall wrappers only where required.\n\nFull TOML parsing uses native `toml` 1.1.8+spec-1.1.0 with its approved `std`/`parse`/`serde` features, grammar, limits and errors. Current Cargo/mise/rust-toolchain/rust-src documents must work. No `unbounded` feature, Python compatibility parser, BigInt emulation, Python recursion/digit/date/NaN/Unicode quirks or diagnostic-order emulation is required. Native serde_json schemas may replace Python encodings; document intentional driver breaks and update callers/tests together. The no-argument installer exposes one fixed SHA-pinned archive, not a caller-supplied archive grammar. Preserve that production universe and every existing installer control. After verifying the archive SHA-256, read the one named regular member in-process with `tar` + `flate2`, then verify the jar SHA-256 before publication. No system tar, member filtering, verbose listing, GNU/BSD parity or platform receipts. Lock with std `File::lock()` (stable since Rust 1.89); keep `O_NOFOLLOW` symlink refusal and regular-file checks.\n\nThe disposable archive experiment's duplicate, type7 and gzip-trailer cases only exist after replacing the pinned archive hash; the archive hash check precedes parsing, so they create no reader-parity obligation. Earlier process/guest work continues independently.\n\nAcceptance checks semantic correctness, safety and deterministic current-input reproduction, not old/new byte or diagnostic equality. Review any necessary guest/golden/pin regeneration, document its cause and old/new hashes, and verify reproducibility plus freshness against the newly committed asset. Prefer one integrated regeneration; repeat only for changed inputs or a specific failed proof. Never use normalization or repinning to hide semantic regressions or weaken mutation/negative controls. Runtime/PID/path/compiler/curl output is checked structurally. Historical Python evidence outside the active toolchain remains history.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- **R1:** `umpire-backend-process` owns the moved support and a Rust supervisor; TLA+ contains no process implementation or Python; TLA+, TLC and Apalache use the new owner without an Apalache-to-TLC dependency. Native caller contracts preserve functional typed failure/status mapping, Java pin, capture/dump/scratch contracts and platform behavior; old signatures, reexports and diagnostic bytes are not required solely for backward compatibility. Errors: unsupported platforms and unsafe Darwin identity refuse at admission; launch, signal, timeout, cancellation and owner EOF controls retain their outcomes; actual Linux direct/escaped descendants are gone before return; output substitution, malformed UTF-8, bounded reads and cleanup failures retain their checks. The unsafe policy compiles without weakening other crates.\n- **R2:** xtask replaces active helper and embedded Python responsibilities with documented native Rust arguments, schemas, grammar, limits and errors; shell/Make callers are updated coherently. Compiler-input identities are deterministic, portable and stable, with new formats/cache invalidation permitted. Necessary guest/golden/pin regeneration records its cause and old/new hashes; rebuilt current-input artifacts are reproducible and freshness passes. Errors: malformed inputs/TOML/JSON/sidecars, missing/ambiguous closures, native-macro collisions, missing/probe-failed tools, stale artifacts, launch/compiler failures, installation hashes/locks/symlinks/concurrency and malformed retired-name streams retain meaningful negative controls. Minimal cold bootstrap disables auto-install before its first probe and fails clearly; Rust owns semantic validation, with no duplicated Bash parser or old CI freshness skip matrix. Python quirks, exact old diagnostics and byte-parity proofs are retired explicitly.\n- **R3:** Every useful existing Python test responsibility and Rust control that constructs Python children has a Rust-owned counterpart; the ordinary gate executes those controls and invokes no Python; no tracked Python file remains under the active Model tree. Record pure Python-quirk assertions retired under the owner amendment rather than recreating them. The byte-only retained-original hash verifier and active hash manifest are removed; semantic lifecycle comparison and its mutation control remain. Errors: a changed original lifecycle edge fails; platform-limited controls are reported honestly; no deletion of a safety control or frozen original satisfies this criterion.\n- **R4:** Current prerequisites, crate/module ownership documentation and the gate describe native Rust driver contracts, intentional CLI/output/parse/error breaks and process platform/safety policy; design evidence records libc/R18 accounting, semantic/control and current-input reproducibility evidence, justified regenerated pins and measured `make model-validate` under R5. Errors: unmet dependency admission remains explicit; missing platform evidence and timing overruns are reported honestly; no historical byte-parity or redundant full-gate receipt is required.\n- **R5:** The canonical `model-validate` entrypoint executes exactly one complete TLC-required Model gate, including all current stages, followed by the existing edited-checker benchmark once. Required Apalache mode remains independently selectable and runs its existing controls inside that same gate. Existing `model-check` and `model-time` commands retain their arguments, mode behavior and failure contracts; focused controls preserve absent/default and explicit-skip semantics. Actual mode, commands, stages and timings are recorded honestly, with required timing identified separately from historical default timing. Errors: conflicting environment/command-line/MAKEFLAGS values cannot turn canonical TLC validation into a skip; gate failure prevents the benchmark; gate and benchmark failures retain both child diagnostic texts and propagate their established statuses at direct-wrapper and GNU Make boundaries; timing overruns remain soft warnings and never hide a failed check; no cached/fabricated pass or omitted stage discharges validation.\n- **R6:** (2026-10-24) The process crate creates scratch directories with `tempfile::Builder` (prefix, 0700, `tempdir_in`, explicit close) and makes parent-side syscalls through `rustix` safe wrappers; the symlink-refusing workspace-relative walk and the forked child's raw-libc path stay. The audited unsafe site count decreases, recorded before/after. Errors: every existing process control (refusal, timeout, cancellation, signal, owner EOF, descendant cleanup, scratch cleanup failure) retains its outcome.\n\n## Parallel implementation and integration join\n\n[direct owner, 2026-10-24] \"can we paralleize more aggressively?\" and \"can we take more risks, too?\" authorize isolated source lanes and deliberate shared-file merge conflicts while retaining correctness, safety and required proofs. After completed .2, launch .5 guest orchestration, .6 installer and .7 retired-name/verifier retirement independently; .9 remains on its existing .4 dependency. No new IDs or requirements are introduced.\n\nEach lane works in an isolated checkout/index and target/cache; no concurrent mutation of shared source/index or shared Cargo/lock/codegen caches. Primary dispatch/finalization is serialized; another isolated source lane may continue while the reviewed checkout remains immutable. Reuse the completed .2 native `Error`/`Result`, `support::{hash,canonical,publish}`, closure and compiler interfaces. .6 owns its tar/flate2 additions in its own Cargo/lock copy; .5 and .7 need no .6 dependency. Each lane may wire its own dispatcher/lib/Makefile copy. The conductor joins those edits centrally, resolves conflicts explicitly, and reruns only focused checks affected by the combined edits. Accepting merge conflicts does not permit dropping another lane's commands, modes, dependencies or safety controls.\n\nLane-local fake-tool/negative controls establish each module's behavior; they do not certify the unmerged combined tool. No lane marks its task done from a local result alone. After the join, run meaningful integrated caller/dispatcher/bootstrap and R18 package/feature/guest-closure checks. .5's final canonical guest/golden/pin publication, two-clean-build reproducibility and committed-asset freshness happen once on the joined inputs under conductor ownership; its worker defers final asset publication and task closure until that proof. Preserve all .5 proof obligations without rebuilding the canonical guest independently in every lane. .6's real pinned-release install and .7's lifecycle comparison/mutation proof run against the joined tool before their completion.\n\nFinal .3 waits explicitly on .2, .5, .6, .7 and .9. It consumes the joined proofs and performs exactly the existing one `UMPIRE_APALACHE=require make model-validate` pipeline and benchmark. No extra full gate is added for parallelism. Completed .1/.2/.4/.8 history and all R1–R6 meanings remain unchanged.\n\n## Early proof point\n\nTask fn-247-one-language-for-the-framework-a-rust.4 proves cold focused-consumer execution plus actual orphan cleanup after .1 pins public process behavior and establishes the extraction. If .4 fails, reconsider the supervisor's fork-safety/production path before porting build tools in .2 and its successors.\n\n## Boundaries\n\nNo Model, DSL, protocol, IR, checker semantics or Temporal feature refactor; only removing the compatibility checkpoint hash-verifier call/manifest reaches that feature scope. Native driver formats and intentional CLI/output/parse/error changes are permitted, with coherent caller/test updates. Necessary guest/golden/pin regeneration requires documented cause, semantic correctness and reproducibility; it does not authorize compiler upgrades or unrelated dependency changes. New dependencies only as admitted above. No frozen-archive or retained-original modification, historical Flow evidence/tooling change, Windows claim, remote operation or history rewrite. Other specs remain separately owned; this amendment changes no scheduling or dependencies.\n\n## Decision Context\n\n2026-10-24, owner: \"can we paralleize more aggressively?\" and \"can we take more risks, too?\". Use isolated concurrent implementation lanes even when Cargo-file edits will conflict; reconcile those copies centrally at integration. Accept rework and narrow inherited-blocker fixes. Keep dependency admission, meaningful correctness/safety checks and reviewed-source immutability; another isolated lane may continue while the main checkout is reviewed.\n\n2026-10-24, [direct owner risk policy, conductor accepted]: \"can we take more risks, too?\" permits ordinary integration conflicts/rework, coordinated breaking changes and narrow inherited-blocker fixes inline. Use isolated lanes while keeping reviewed-source immutability, dependency admission, correctness/security/process safety, scientific oracle independence and final joined proof. Prefer focused checks over redundant validations; this policy waives no meaningful negative control or final acceptance evidence.\n\nMaintainability (plan review): duplication - Task .3 would duplicate process/xtask controls between the replaced unittest stage and retained workspace stage; resolved by assigning them exclusively to the workspace stage. structure - none identified. Shared Cargo ownership for .9/.2/.6 is explicitly serialized while preserving independent source lanes.\n\n\n2026-10-24, [user-approved savings]: \"yes apply savings\" authorizes R5 and changes R4's final measurement command from `model-time` to `model-validate`. Reuse the existing timing orchestration for one required full gate and one edited-checker benchmark. Preserve legacy commands and all coverage, use focused intermediate checks, and keep root-owned milestone bookkeeping frozen during validation. Required timings are not directly comparable with historical default-gate timings. No TLC batching, caching, parallelism or new report framework is included.\n\n[conductor clarification] `model-validate` forces TLC but leaves Apalache independently opt-in. The new prerequisite verifies the TLC-required default once; final .3 explicitly opts into both backends in its 