nds/process/src/syscall.rs\": {\n      \"sha256\": \"195b054420b14d273049aeb82f1e60501d876e36ebf07170c691ca0a818e8a90\",\n      \"names\": [\n        \"duplicated_descriptors_are_private_and_close_on_exec\",\n        \"close_limit_retains_descriptors_above_lowered_soft_limit\",\n        \"supervisor_owner_eof_returns_125\",\n        \"supervisor_retains_first_stop_signal\",\n        \"all_darwin_helpers_prevent_process_creation\",\n        \"unsupported_platform_is_an_explicit_refusal\"\n      ],\n      \"count\": \"source declarations, not executed results\",\n      \"declaration_count\": 6\n    }\n  },\n  \"lockfile_package_count\": 152,\n  \"draft_make_removed_python_reference\": true,\n  \"native_test_stage_exactly_once\": true,\n  \"source_state\": \"task3 committed source; full source-binding.json binds the final input set\",\n  \"python_token_scan\": [\n    {\n      \"path\": \"model/dev/xtask/src/support.rs\",\n      \"line\": 11,\n      \"text\": \"/// Native identity and sidecar version. Old Python records are deliberately rejected.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"path\": \"model/umpire/backends/process/tests/process.rs\",\n      \"line\": 66,\n      \"text\": \"fn cold_native_execution_does_not_need_python_or_a_sibling_supervisor() -> Result<(), Refusal> {\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"scan_classification\": \"Two descriptive tokens: native-schema documentation and no-Python process control name. No executable invocation/heredoc. Make model stages checked separately.\",\n  \"final_source_hashes\": {\n    \"Makefile\": \"261f445547de8e8c8aa3f41a8de65b7e4316a45ebcf3d8e70c8cb9c3b30c63a4\",\n    \"model/README.md\": \"3cdec2ea92a2968fc995a37bcee5ea5f560d033280906d541cf47389d16f439c\",\n    \"model/GUEST_BUILDS.md\": \"3504a1ab98246d63c15c49af0cf294075624cbcf8a0f4bb7ec7920b235271ced\",\n    \"model/DESIGN.md\": \"33051f7094c375979b1d367d13af51104836b820b87cdd8d97d886cf4f98bc10\",\n    \".plans/UMPIRE_MODULES.md\": \"e51386bd78f062a043c36e6912a1656ba4c4025a6fcc99e46dd4961034b76cb0\"\n  }\n}\n# Task .9 parent-safe dependency adoption\n\nScratch now privately owns a `tempfile::TempDir`, creates `run-` leaves through `Builder::permissions(0700).tempdir_in`, exposes `path()`, and consumes its owner with explicit `close()` in `finish`. The workspace-relative component walk is unchanged. Successful cleanup preserves the original success or failure; cleanup failure still reports the prior error. TempDir provides best-effort Drop cleanup. As documented by tempfile, failed explicit close consumes ownership without an automatic second cleanup attempt; its failure stays visible. No keep/into_path/manual ownership shortcut is used. TLC and Apalache changes only migrate Scratch path access.\n\nParent process identity, descriptor duplication, resource limits, exact-child polling/blocking waits, fixture parent/group identity, fixture subreaper admission and all-owned-child reaping now use rustix. `Running` stores a positive typed Pid. Exact-child waits pass `Some(pid)`; the isolated fixture's any-child reaper uses `wait(empty)`, because `waitpid(None)` only selects the caller's process group. EINTR retry, exit-status conversion/cache and owner-pipe-before-wait Drop ordering are retained. Descriptor duplication still requests minimum10 and CLOEXEC. The optional/infinite or overflowing soft limit saturates at i32::MAX; existing `/dev/fd` enumeration still includes descriptors above a lowered soft limit.\n\n## Scope and remaining raw calls\n\nThe source baseline is `fb25091a0e73d3c25a2569cd811f78ac31d2eb5f`. Similar functionality was extended in the existing process owner; no competing scratch or process abstraction was introduced. The preparation research and three process/capture memories supplied the boundary constraints. All source changes are within process, the explicitly authorized mechanical TLC/Apalache callers and workspace dependency declarations/lock edges. Formatting two unrelated xtask files was incidental to cargo fmt --all; their exact baseline bytes were restored and they are absent from this task's diff. Final formatting checks are scoped to the three owned consumers. No Flow lifecycle/review state or feature map was changed.\n\nRemaining unsafe/raw operations have specific boundary reasons:\n\n- Raw clone/fork and the transfer to child_main preserve the reviewed cancellation/atfork bypass and architecture-specific s390x/SPARC contracts. A general safe fork wrapper is not equivalent.\n- Parent preparation of libc sigaction/sigset structures and SignalMask block/restore preserve the exact kernel signal representation, reserved-signal blocking and eager binding used by the raw child. They cannot be independently replaced with a general wrapper without changing that shared contract.\n- calls::Api::new resolves the audited libc symbols, cancellation-free Darwin entries, errno/TLS and transitive signal/cancellation bindings before fork. Its unsafe ABI work remains necessary to prepare the existing child table.\n- The complete forked supervisor, target and transitive cleanup/error paths remain prepared raw calls: child subreaper, wait/reap loops, signals, descriptor closure, procfs reads, NPROC, exec fallback and _exit. No rustix/tempfile/std/allocator/lock/TLS lookup/unwind/destructor was introduced after fork.\n- restrict_descendants intentionally exercises the same raw NPROC helper used by the production target; converting this fixture alone would weaken its proof. poison_atfork_child intentionally installs a raw pthread_atfork callback and _exit control.\n\n`child-boundary.json` verifies byte identity for the production region from SignalMask through restrict_descendants, and whole-file identity for syscall/calls.rs. Prepared executable fallback and clone argument/result helpers remain in that identical region. The source hashes are in `verified-source-hashes.json`.\n\n## Unsafe inventory\n\nThe same lexical algorithm over every process src/**/*.rs, including all cfg branches and inline tests, counts unsafe blocks (`unsafe\\s*\\{`) and named unsafe function definitions (`unsafe\\s+(?:extern\\s+\"[^\"]+\"\\s+)?fn\\s+[\\w$]+\\s*\\(`). This includes the macro template unsafe method, but excludes function-pointer types and unsafe extern declarations. Matched entries were inspected as actual code, rather than comments/string false positives. This is source accounting, not macro-expanded or runtime/architecture coverage.\n\nBefore:52 blocks+25 definitions=77. After:42 blocks+25 definitions=67. All10 removed blocks were actual parent operations: UID1, try_wait1, Drop wait1, fcntl/ownership2, getrlimit/initialization2, fixture parent identity1, fixture subreaper1, fixture reaping1. syscall.rs falls36→26 blocks with15 definitions unchanged; calls.rs retains16 blocks+10 definitions. No unsafe blocks were grouped, moved, hidden behind new macros, cfg-trimmed or reclassified to lower the count. Safe support/tests remain unsafe-free; workspace forbid and crate-local deny/syscall-only allow are unchanged. Both inventories retain per-file source hashes and line-level sites.\n\n## Dependency and guest accounting\n\nBoth crates were already resolved. Direct declarations pin rustix=1.1.5 with only its default std support plus process, and tempfile=3.27.0 with ordinary default random naming. The lock diff adds only rustix/tempfile to the process crate's dependency list. Fresh fixed-scope offline locked metadata has145 package identities before and145 after, with zero added/removed identities. Historical137-package research preceded the already integrated xtask foundation and is not this resolver receipt.\n\nThe complete raw metadata and `dependency-inventory.json` retain package/version/source, feature and edge data. Only global rustix/process and linux-raw-sys/prctl features were added. Normal-edge topology for all six guest roots is identical before/after. This metadata union includes proc-macro host graphs; it is not a compiled runtime closure. Separate target-specific wasm32-wasip1 cargo trees for all six roots contain neither the process crate nor rustix's process feature. Existing tempfile/rustix fs reachability through prost-build/catalog proc-macro host tools remains visible and is not called a new guest runtime dependency. No guest, DSL, Model, protocol or semantic source changed. Final integrated guest reproduction remains task .3's obligation.\n\n## Verification and limits\n\nBaseline: green,25 process tests and scoped process Clippy before source changes. Final focused observations are29 process tests (9 unit+20 integration),21 TLC/Apalache library tests, one real TLC typed initial-counterexample control and one real Apalache postcondition-filter control (three actual checker runs):52 distinct test identities. Process and both backend all-target Clippy and scoped format checks pass. `commands.json` records exact invocations, exit codes, output paths and failures; raw suite logs are retained. The empty doc-test selection is not counted as coverage. No full Model gate, timing triplicate, review verdict or Flow done was run here.\n\nThe unchanged process controls retain timeout/cancellation output, owner EOF125, group/direct-supervisor signal status, Linux direct/escaped descendant disappearance before fallback, launch/environment/cwd/argv, pthread-atfork exclusion, concurrent allocators/spawning, Java admission/environment, inode-held capture, symlink/FIFO rejection, combined budget/UTF8 and dump/unsafe scratch failures. Existing missing-directory cleanup control still checks both cleanup failure and prior failure. New controls check TempDir uniqueness/private mode/explicit and Drop cleanup/result retention, descriptor numbering/CLOEXEC, a lowered soft limit with an open high descriptor, and cross-process-group reaping. The reaping control failed under an intentional waitpid(None) mutant, then passed with wait(empty). The Scratch path test was compile-red before the new accessor; its behavior assertions passed after adoption, and it is not presented as a behavior-red proof. Other compile and Clippy iteration failures are retained honestly.\n\nNative scope is Linux/aarch64, glibc2.43, uid/euid1000, rustc1.99.1. Darwin admission/simulated restriction and s390x/SPARC scalar controls are not native execution receipts for those platforms. Infinity/overflow limit saturation is a source-level Option/conversion audit; the lowered-limit case is executed in an isolated test process. The two jar files were copied as regular files from the conductor-approved existing cache into private workspace cache parents; SHA-256 matches both backend pins and actual discovery/version admission passed with mise auto-install disabled. No download, symlink bypass or shared target was used. Java heap size remains distinct from an OS memory cap; regular-file polling remains distinct from a strict kernel output cap.\n\nstage: impl-review - skipped(policy: parallel-wave - conductor owns review and done)\nTier: session (jev-unavailable(no_key))\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
