{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_43","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '245,290p' model/DESIGN.md; sed -n '1,77p' model/GUEST_BUILDS.md; cat .flow/artifacts/fn-247-one-language-for-the-framework-a-rust/task3/control-mapping.md; cat .flow/artifacts/fn-247-one-language-for-the-framework-a-rust/task5/review/guest-proof-continuity.json\"","aggregated_output":"## Native framework tools (fn-247)\n\nThe owner moved bounded process ownership out of the TLA+ emitter and removed Python\nfrom active Model tooling. `umpire-backend-process` owns Java admission, capture/dump\nchecks, scratch cleanup and supervision for TLC/Apalache. Parent preparation resolves\nall executable/argv/environment/descriptor state. The forked supervisor/target uses\nthe audited allocation-free syscall path, with no runtime Cargo or sibling supervisor.\nLinux subreaper cleanup includes escaped descendants; Darwin refuses root and prevents\ndescendant creation. Other platforms refuse. Linux controls and Darwin source/simulation\nare recorded separately; no native macOS process proof is claimed.\n\nThe process crate's audited syscall exception retains the workspace forbid elsewhere.\nrustix handles parent-side safe operations; tempfile owns private scratch with explicit\nobservable close. Task9's lexical unsafe inventory decreased from 77 to 67 sites; this\nis a source count, not a proof of runtime safety. Java heap configuration is not an OS\nmemory cap, and regular-file polling is not a strict kernel output cap.\n\nHost-only `model/dev/xtask` owns native guest/closure/compiler identity/freshness,\nApalache installation and retired-name filtering. Bash bootstraps the native tool\nbefore guest wrappers/flags and disables automatic installs before its first probe.\nRust owns TOML/JSON/pin/readiness admission, including cold and cached paths; missing\ntools fail regardless of CI. Schema 2 and native parser/argument/error contracts replace\nold Python encodings and quirks, with coordinated callers and cache invalidation.\nRaw OS argv, prior wrappers, jobserver/loader inheritance and sidecar-before-compiler\npublication/failure cleanup remain controls. [Native contracts](dev/xtask/README.md).\n\nR18 admissions are libc 0.2.191, rustix 1.1.5, tempfile 3.27.0, native toml 1.1.8+spec-1.1.0\n(std/parse/serde), sha2 0.11.1, tar 0.4.46 (no default/xattr) and flate2 1.1.10 (miniz_oxide).\nThe [dependency inventory](../.flow/artifacts/fn-247-one-language-for-the-framework-a-rust/task6/dependency-inventory.json)\nrecords 145→152 packages and the seven archive transitive/direct additions; retained\nR18 evidence accounts for each. Before/after six-guest closure maps agree across the\ninstaller additions; they do not substitute for compilation. [Joined install evidence](../.flow/artifacts/fn-247-one-language-for-the-framework-a-rust/task6/joined/README.md)\nrecords actual fixed-release admission and cached reuse. Archive SHA precedes parsing;\nthe exact regular member and jar hash precede locked atomic publication. No arbitrary\narchive input, system tar or reader-parity contract is introduced.\n\nThe checksum-only original verifier/manifest is removed; historical originals, semantic\nlifecycle comparison and the changed-edge mutation control remain. Historical Python\ncertification helpers under `.flow/artifacts` remain evidence, not active prerequisites.\nThe ordinary workspace Rust test stage owns native controls once. Component receipts,\nthe necessary native-identity guest regeneration and Linux two-clone proof are retained\nunder [fn247 evidence](../.flow/artifacts/fn-247-one-language-for-the-framework-a-rust/).\nFinal task3 owns one combined required `model-validate` gate plus its existing benchmark;\ncomponent and historical default timings do not certify that final integrated run.\n\n## Reproducible guest builds (fn-230)\n# Guest builds: reproduction and diagnostics\n\n`bash model/scripts/guest.sh <crate> <output> [--check]` builds any cdylib with\nthe mise-pinned Rust 1.99.1 compiler, `--locked`, `wasm32-wasip1` and `profile.guest`.\nIt discovers the output through Cargo's artifact messages, including custom library\nnames. It requires Bash, mise, installed pinned Rust/Cargo, and the `rust-src` component;\nactive guest/build helpers require no Python.\n`xtask.sh` builds the current native host tool in `<target>/xtask-bootstrap` before\ninstalling guest wrappers, flags or cache settings. It prints the absolute binary\npath for shell callers. This incremental build also prevents stale cached tools\nfrom bypassing source changes. Whitespace in checkout and tool paths is supported.\nThe atomic `guest-rustc` launcher is an executable copy of that exact native binary,\nselected by its executable name; it preserves macOS loader variables and inherited\njobserver handles without a shebang, `/usr/bin/env` or recursive Cargo invocation.\nExit 0 means built or fresh, 1 means native tool/input/output failure, and 2 means\nmissing or stale output. Guest compiler exit codes pass through (signals use 128 +\nsignal); their diagnostics remain on stderr. Staleness reports existing/built sizes and the first differing\nzero-based byte offset, including EOF; check mode never updates the output.\nRelative output paths are relative to the caller. `CARGO_TARGET_DIR` selects the\nbuild cache, defaulting to `model/target`. Guest compilation uses a versioned\n`guest-cache/<identity>` subdirectory beneath it; artifact destinations are unchanged.\n\nThe profile inherits release, strips symbols, disables debug information and uses\nfat LTO with one codegen unit and checked overflow. The checker's release profile\nkeeps its profiling symbols. Workspace, Cargo registry, toolchain sysroot and target\npaths are remapped by rustc, including generated protobuf locations under `OUT_DIR`.\nNo wasm bytes are rewritten after linking. The Temporal API generator sorts merged\ndescriptor files before prost generation; otherwise Go map iteration could change\nmerged Rust declaration order and the resulting guest bytes.\n\n`xtask guest-rustc` derives target symbol identities from the pinned compiler, the\ncrate's own lockfile closure, normalized package/source paths, crate configuration\nand dependency identities. Before building, `xtask guest-lock` digests, for every\nworkspace-resolved package, the lockfile coordinates and checksums of its non-dev\ndependency closure across all platforms; path packages carry no location. The map\nis written as `umpire-lock-<content hash>.json` in the guest cache. Adding an\nunrelated workspace crate, or a dev-dependency, changes no guest bytes unless it\nchanges the resolved dependencies of a package in a guest's closure. `cargo metadata`\nresolves optional dependencies across the whole workspace, so enabling an optional\ndependency or feature of a shared crate still changes the guest. This over-hashes but\nnever under-hashes; the build's own `--unit-graph` would be an exact per-unit\nrefinement. Packages that share a name and version across sources, or lack a closure,\nrefuse the build, as does a registry package without a lockfile entry. Build-std units\ncarry Cargo's std marker and come from the sysroot or from registry crates in the\nsysroot's own `library/Cargo.lock`; they take no workspace closure, since the pinned\ncompiler fixes theirs.\nCargo retains its own filenames and fingerprints. Atomic per-artifact `.umpire-id`\nfiles carry the portable identities; missing or malformed records fail the build.\nRecords appear before rustc starts because Cargo pipelines dependent compilations\nafter metadata emission, and compiler failures remove them. Host proc macros compile\nunchanged; their portable package/feature/profile identities include collision\nwitnesses that refuse conflicting native variants in one target dependency graph.\nEach target's `OUT_DIR` remaps to `/generated/<portable identity>`, removing Cargo's\nhost-specific directory hash from generated-source locations. Native tool binary,\nlockfile or compiler changes select a new cache so older artifacts cannot bypass this\nevidence; a lockfile change outside every guest closure rebuilds identical bytes.\nExisting `RUSTC_WRAPPER` executables are chained. These inputs support the pinned\nworkspace and toolchain. Changed compiler/dependency inputs require current-input\nreproduction, freshness and existing host controls. Platform evidence is explicit;\nhistorical cross-OS certification is not a compatibility gate for the native driver.\n\n\nNative guest commands execute with `model/` as their working directory:\n`xtask guest <crate> <output> [--check]` and\n`xtask guest-freshness <crate> <output>`. Shell entrypoints preserve caller-relative\noutput paths; relative target/cache paths retain their model-relative meaning.\nCargo artifact input is UTF-8 newline-delimited JSON with a string `reason`;\n`compiler-artifact` records require a typed `target.crate_types` array and\n`filenames` array. Exactly one cdylib `.wasm` path is accepted, including custom\nlibrary names. Missing, multiple, malformed or unreadable artifacts fail before\npublication. Successful builds publish by sibling rename; checks never publish.\nThe native v2 closure/sidecar schemas intentionally invalidate Python records.\nJSON whitespace, Python TOML quirks, exact old diagnostics and historical guest\nbytes are not compatibility contracts. `RUSTFLAGS` accepts whitespace-separated\nUTF-8 tokens; `CARGO_ENCODED_RUSTFLAGS` preserves raw OS tokens separated by 0x1f.\n\n## Unwinding on the pinned compiler\n# Historical Python responsibilities → current native owners\n\nFinal task3 source census. Historical inventory anchor `94e47d7878` contains 14 tracked Python files; current `git ls-files model` contains zero. The frozen archive had none at that historical anchor. `census.json` records the exact old paths, current source declarations/hashes and executable-token scan. This is source ownership evidence, not a new test run.\n\n| Historical path under model/ | Current owner / selected controls | Explicitly retired behavior |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| scripts/guest-lock.py | xtask `src/lock.rs`; `guest_identity` → identity/lock: checksum/all-platform/build-edge propagation, edge topology/unification, missing/sourced/ambiguous coordinates, portability and atomic publication | Old JSON/hash schema, json.dumps spacing/escaping, Python TOML grammar/BigInt/date/recursion/digit/Unicode/error quirks |\n| scripts/tests/test_guest_lock.py | Same native lock corpus; task2/control-ledger.md maps every retained family | Python import/patch harness and exact exception/diagnostic bytes |\n| scripts/guest-rustc.py | xtask `src/compiler.rs`; identity/compiler: raw OS argv/roots, sidecar-before-child, prior wrapper/loader/jobserver, build-std, macro collision, launch/compiler/signal failure | Schema1 sidecars/identity bytes and Python launcher/shebang limitations |\n| scripts/tests/test_guest_rustc.py | `guest_identity`, including actual native rustc macro and inherited FD controls; identity/support adds sorted JSON/hash/publication controls | Python executable/import fixtures and formatting quirks |\n| scripts/guest-freshness.py | xtask `src/guest.rs`; guest_pipeline `cold_shell_bootstrap_and_cached_native_validation`, `native_prerequisite_failures_stop_before_guest_build`, `rust_toolchain_metadata_and_rustup_failures_refuse` | Local warning/skip vs CI missing-tool matrix; now missing tools fail for absent/empty/nonempty CI |\n| scripts/tests/test_guest_freshness.py | Native guest_pipeline cold bootstrap/failure/readiness corpus and actual Make freshness recipe stale control | Old Python metadata/error rendering and repeated Bash semantic validation |\n| scripts/tests/test_guest.py | guest_pipeline artifact zero/multiple/malformed refusal, caller-relative paths/cache changes, never-write check, absent embedded asset Go-compilation negative control | Python heredoc mocks; native statuses/CLI/schema replace exact old formatting |\n| scripts/fetch-apalache.py | xtask `src/fetch_apalache.rs`/`apalache_pins.rs`; `apalache_install` regular member/hash/lock/containment/private staging/concurrency controls | Python error/import/patch mechanisms; arbitrary mock-pinned archive-reader parity was never production input scope |\n| scripts/tests/test_apalache.py | Eight named `tests/apalache/cases.rs` controls; task6/controls-and-handover.md maps symlinks/partial/wrong jars, malicious locks, hash-before-parse, curl failure, real child concurrency and native CLI | No useful installer safety control retired; only Python quirks/byte output |\n| scripts/retired-name-matches.py | xtask `src/retired_names.rs`; raw NUL records/non-UTF8, author-token boundaries, mixed forbidden tokens and external POSIX grep recheck | Python Unicode whitespace classification; native ASCII/byte grammar is documented |\n| scripts/tests/test_retired_names.py | Seven current `retired_names` controls, including actual shell filter-error vs git SIGPIPE precedence added at c0a76a4749 | Python interpreter/errors; grep 0/1/error and stream failure controls remain |\n| scripts/tests/test_backend_process.py | process integration/syscall controls: unsupported/Darwin admission, no-descendant prevention, private group/owner EOF/signal outcomes, Linux escaped descendants and threaded allocation/atfork controls | Python ctypes/mock implementation; native Darwin execution is not inferred from simulation/source checks |\n| umpire/backends/tla/src/process.py | process `src/syscall.rs` + `syscall/calls.rs`, parent-safe support and Rust fixture; cold native consumer requires no Python/runtime Cargo/sibling helper; task4/control-parity.json maps retained controls | Python supervisor runtime/bootstrap and old diagnostic/API byte promises; functional statuses/safety remain |\n| temporal/feature/activity/compatibility/verify.py | Byte-only verifier and active manifest removed; checkpoint retains `retained_original_exact_lifecycle_projection` and `changed_original_lifecycle_edge_fails_the_same_comparison` | Exact15 checksum/commit/count integrity gate explicitly dropped by owner; originals and independent semantic/mutation proof remain |\n\nAdditional embedded Python: guest.sh's cache key, launcher and artifact-selection heredocs are now native xtask responsibilities; Rust process tests now use the compiled Rust process fixture. The obsolete Make unittest discovery stage has no surviving Python source/control module and is removed. Native control ownership is the existing `cargo test --workspace --locked` stage once, not another package pass. Timing/Bash capabilities/default/skip controls from task8 remain separate responsibilities and existing stage ordering is otherwise preserved.\n\n## Evidence bounds\n\n- task2/control-ledger.md records lock/compiler control transfer and retired Python quirks; its intermediate statement that Python callers still exist is historical task2 state, superseded by tasks5–7.\n- task4 evidence records 25 focused process tests and 48 distinct process/backend test identities at that source, with actual Linux orphan/allocator/atfork/cold checks and source/simulation-only Darwin/non-host layout evidence. task9 later owns parent-safe rustix/tempfile changes; its lexical unsafe inventory is 77→67, not a runtime certificate.\n- task5/joined/proof-report.md records two independent clean Linux source/target builds, cold/cached freshness and expected stale status2. canonical-publication.json later records actual publication/checks, 3507265 bytes and SHA bc27af7d5b6b1ed8fa5c524015046f3a8b7c12d2d488e9ce7cb3df3ea9217872. The earlier report's publication-pending prose remains historical; no edit to that evidence is proposed.\n- task6/joined/README.md records actual official pinned-release installation and cached reuse, plus unchanged six-guest closure maps. Package inventory is 145→152; seven additions are adler2, crc32fast, filetime, flate2, miniz_oxide, simd-adler32 and tar. Current lock has 152 packages. These inventory/install proofs are not semantic backend/Go executions.\n- integration/joined-validation.json records **81** xtask+Apalache tests at source523d131, two lifecycle controls, clippy and actual cold retired scan. Later review fixes add the seventh retired-name control and ninth guest-pipeline control; the historical 81 result is not a current total. task7 review artifacts contain its later focused results. Final task3 reports actual final workspace selections/results rather than summing overlapping historical receipts.\n- task8's 1007.800s gate / 9.272s benchmark and 720-passed workspace summary belong to its earlier TLC-required-only source. Do not relabel those as the new combined TLC/Apalache-required final run. Its mode/failure/soft-budget controls remain meaningful.\n\nOnly the final task3-owned `UMPIRE_APALACHE=require make model-validate` discharges the pending complete semantic Go/TLC/Apalache pipeline. It runs once on the committed task3 integration, then its existing benchmark. Final task3 commands/results are recorded separately in validation.json and model-validate.log.\n{\n  \"proof_source\": \"523d131d2cf992b04a43b4d595ea0f1a434f44b6\",\n  \"model_make_modulemap_diff\": [\n    \"model/dev/xtask/tests/guest_pipeline.rs\",\n    \"model/dev/xtask/tests/identity/compiler_fixture.rs\",\n    \"model/dev/xtask/tests/retired_names.rs\",\n    \"model/scripts/retired-names.sh\",\n    \"model/temporal/hosts/go/umpire_temporal_guest.wasm\"\n  ],\n  \"production_guest_build_compiler_identity_inputs_unchanged\": true,\n  \"root_authority\": \"Conductor explicitly approved test-only native fixture correction and confirmed proof remains valid without regeneration/full-gate repeat.\",\n  \"current_sha256\": {\n    \"model/dev/xtask/tests/guest_pipeline.rs\": \"472bbe7a9bcd4a2cd6cc47791a3850becfca7199bda73099d0e9cc3856bb5208\",\n    \"model/dev/xtask/tests/identity/compiler_fixture.rs\": \"5a36ee9ba6f6b95c